/*  The build description for the snake game.

    ★★ Running this performs NOTHING. It defines `blueprint` and calls nothing, so what comes out
    is a plan; the build happens afterwards, to that plan. `cufet blueprint.cufe` printing no
    output is correct rather than a bug — which is also what lets this file sit in the corpus and
    be run on both backends like every other program here.

    ★★ **`needs` is GENERATED, not hand-kept.** `cufet pulls tools/snake/snake.cufe` prints what
    the loader actually resolved, and that is where the list below came from — the tool exists so
    a blueprint need not track dependencies by hand, and it reports what a build really loads
    rather than a re-reading of the source, so it cannot drift from the truth. Re-run it after
    adding or removing a pull.

    ★ Pulls and `needs` answer DIFFERENT questions, which is why both exist. A `Pull` says what
    code is part of the program — it is the only linking mechanism, since there is no separate
    compilation. `needs` says what files, if they change, mean this step must run again; staleness
    is decided by hashing them, so the list has to be declared whatever the pulls say. They
    happen to be the same list, which is exactly what lets one generate the other.

    ⚠ Paths are relative to the PROJECT ROOT — the directory holding this file — not to wherever
    `cufet build` was invoked from. Writing them repo-relative produced
    `tools/snake/tools/snake/snake.cufe`, which is how this was learned.

    ⚠ The graph is never written down. A step runs after another when it NEEDS a path that one
    MAKES; declaring edges as well would state one fact twice. There is only one step here, so
    nothing yet depends on that.
*/

Pull a book on blueprints.
    Bind series of step to blueprint:
        Define work as a series of step.

        Insert a record with (
            the name "snake",
            the needs a series of text with ("snake.cufe", "screen.cufe", "board.cufe", "pacing.cufe"),
            the makes a series of text with ("snake.exe"),
            the runs a series of text with ("cufet", "build", "snake.cufe")) into work.

        Return work.
    Done.
Done.
